---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifePairConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifePairConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="kg-to-lbs">
  <UnitsLifePairConverter client:load spec="kg-to-lbs" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The pound is a metric unit too</h2>
    <p>
      Since 1959 the international avoirdupois pound has been defined as
      <strong>exactly 0.45359237 kilograms</strong>. So kg to lbs is a division by that number, or a
      multiplication by 2.2046226218, and the result is exact. No more accurate figure exists, because
      this is the definition.
    </p>
    <p>
      The familiar "2.2" shortcut is 0.2% low. On a 70 kg body weight that is 340 g, which does not
      matter. On a 20-tonne shipment it is 44 kg, which does.
    </p>

    <h2>Three different answers for the same weight</h2>
    <p>
      70 kg can be written in all of these ways, and different countries expect different ones:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Form</th><th scope="col">70 kg is</th><th scope="col">Used in</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Kilograms</td><td>70 kg</td><td>Almost everywhere, and all medical records</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pounds</td><td>154.32 lb</td><td>United States</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stone and pounds</td><td>11 st 0.3 lb</td><td>United Kingdom, Ireland</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pounds and ounces</td><td>154 lb 5.2 oz</td><td>Parcels, small goods, birth weights</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The page shows all four at once, because the arithmetic usually goes wrong when you convert to
      decimal pounds and then calculate stone by hand.
    </p>

    <h2>Weights worth knowing by heart</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>1 kg = 2.20 lb &middot; 5 kg = 11.02 lb &middot; 10 kg = 22.05 lb</li>
      <li>20 kg = 44.09 lb, the common checked-bag allowance</li>
      <li>23 kg = 50.71 lb, the other common allowance. So the US "50 lb" limit is slightly lower</li>
      <li>45 kg = 99.21 lb &middot; 100 kg = 220.46 lb</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Gym plates never quite match</h2>
    <p>
      An Olympic bar is 20 kg (44.09 lb) in metric gyms and 45 lb (20.41 kg) in American ones. A
      20 kg plate is 44.09 lb, and a 45 lb plate is 20.41 kg. So a bar loaded from a mixed set is
      slightly unequal, and a "225 lb" bench in an American gym is 102.06 kg, not the 100 kg a metric
      lifter would call it. Personal records do not convert cleanly, so record which set you used.
    </p>

    <h2>Mass, weight and force</h2>
    <p>
      This converter handles mass. The pound is also a unit of force (pound-force): the weight of a
      pound-mass under standard gravity. On Earth the two are numerically equal, so the difference does
      not matter in daily life. But for a spring rating, a thrust figure or a torque, check which pound
      you have before you apply 0.45359237.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting a spreadsheet column</h2>
    <p>
      Switch to the Batch tab, paste the column, and copy the result. Blank lines stay, so the result
      pastes back aligned. Lines the tool cannot read come back empty, with no substitute value. All
      work happens in this browser tab.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
